::ALG2_U04_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which table shows exponential behavior? {
= 1, 3, 9, 27#Correct. The outputs multiply by 3 each step, so the pattern is exponential.
~ 2, 5, 8, 11#This pattern adds 3 each step, so it is linear.
~ 4, 9, 14, 19#This pattern adds 5 each step, so it is linear.
~ 10, 8, 6, 4#This pattern subtracts 2 each step, so it is linear.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>In f(x) = 6(4)<sup>x</sup>, what is the initial value? {
~ 4#This is the base, not the initial value.
= 6#Correct. The initial value is a, the output when x = 0.
~ 10#This adds the two parameters instead of identifying the initial value.
~ 24#This is f(1), not f(0).
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>A pattern starts at 5 and triples each step. Which function models it? {
~ f(x) = 3(5)<sup>x</sup>#This reverses the initial value and base.
~ f(x) = 5 + 3x#This models adding 3, not multiplying by 3.
= f(x) = 5(3)<sup>x</sup>#Correct. The initial value is 5 and the repeated factor is 3.
~ f(x) = 15x#This is linear and does not include repeated multiplication.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>What is f(3) for f(x) = 2(3)<sup>x</sup>? {
~ 18#This uses 3 squared instead of 3 cubed.
~ 27#This finds only 3 cubed and ignores the initial factor 2.
~ 162#This multiplies by an extra factor of 3.
= 54#Correct. Compute 2 times 3 cubed, so 2 times 27 = 54.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>A population begins at 80 and doubles every hour. Which model fits? {
= P(t) = 80(2)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. The initial amount is 80 and doubling means the base is 2.
~ P(t) = 2(80)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses the initial amount and base.
~ P(t) = 80 + 2t#This models adding 2 each hour, not doubling.
~ P(t) = 160t#This is linear and does not represent repeated doubling.
}

